    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Morgan Rae Playle was 27 when she “stepped into” adulthood. She started using uppercase letters in her texts."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Turning on "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "auto-capitalisation",
            "zh": "自动大写"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " for online messages has become a symbol of adulthood in the West. We all know that sentences should start with a capital letter. Names and other "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "proper nouns",
            "zh": "专有名词"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " get the same treatment. The question for many young people now is: When will society expect me to start following these grammar rules?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Playle typed in lowercase throughout her twenties. She used to work online and made it a habit to send messages without caps. But last November, Playle started a new job. She didn’t want her clients to see all her messages in lowercase and think she was an "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "intern",
            "zh": "实习生"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". That was why she turned on auto-capitalisation. “One "
          },
          {
            "t": "underline",
            "v": "chapter"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " of my life has closed,” says Playle. “I’ve grown out of the all-lowercase age.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Emilie Mosner is an 18-year-old high school student. She turned off auto-capitalisation on her phone when she was in ninth grade. Now that she is applying for part-time jobs, she decided it was time to turn it back on. Some of her friends wondered why she was suddenly sounding so serious. When Mosner explained herself, they, too, realised they’d soon be ending their lowercase lives."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Some people see typing in all lowercase as a sign of laziness, but language researchers disagree. Lowercase typing actually takes effort since auto-capitalisation is usually on by "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "default",
            "ref": "v-default"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Like "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "emojis",
            "ref": "v-emoji"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", lowercase typing is meant to show familiarity. It’s one way people try to get their humanness to shine in the cold world of technology."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "These days, many young adults well into their thirties are sticking to lowercase. Some popular young artists now have lowercase song titles. Sam Altman, C.E.O. of OpenAI, is known for sending most of his messages in lowercase. His "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "avoidance",
            "ref": "v-avoidance"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " of the uppercase hasn’t stopped him from running one of the most powerful companies in the world."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Drew Coffman, 35, stopped using proper capitalisation around 2021. “It just "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "hit",
            "ref": "v-hit"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " me that I don’t have to try to be overly polite all the time,” he says. “There are a lot of advantages to being a human being online.”"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
